his massive frame parting the fleeing patrons with little effort.

The morning star begins its descent.

But he is not alone.

The tavern door opens for a second time tonight.

A far smaller figure slips quietly inside, unnoticed amidst the chaos. A slender blade rests at his waist in a scuffed leather scabbard.

No burst of wind this time. No dramatic silence. The stranger simply walks in, shakes the rain from his shoulders like an old hound, and mutters an apology to the puddle he leaves on the floorboards.

He is short. Noticeably so—the top of his hood barely clears the belt buckles of the men he squeezes past. His clothes are dark but plain: travel-worn wool, mud-flecked boots, a slender blade resting at his waist in a scuffed leather scabbard. He looks, at first glance, like a courier. A clerk, perhaps. Someone whose troubles end at dusk.

Then the lantern light catches his face as he passes, and the first glance quietly stops being trustworthy.

His shadow stretches the wrong way—too long for his frame, reaching for the hearth like it knows the fire personally. And for half a heartbeat, his eyes hold a pale shimmer, as if something on the other side of them is looking back.

The stranger notices you noticing. He smiles warmly, raises a hand in a small apologetic wave, and mouths sorry—for what, he doesn't specify.

Then the morning star falls.

Steel screams across the elf's blade and hurls her against a table. She rolls through shattered mugs and rising bodies, dagger already spinning back into her grip. The giant advances, indifferent to the chaos, customers scrambling from his path like water around a stone.

The short stranger weaves toward the fight instead of away from it.

"Excuse me. Terribly sorry—coming through," he murmurs, quiet enough that you have to strain to hear it. stepping over spilled ale and unconscious patrons as if attending a dinner party.

He reaches the duel just as the morning star rises again.

"Good evening," he calls, his voice carrying lightly through the room as he steps between the warhammer and the fallen elf. "Quite a storm outside. Though it seems the thunder found its way indoors."

The morning star pauses mid-swing.

The giant blinks. "Move, little man."

"I'd rather not," the stranger says pleasantly. And then, softer, almost as if remembering something painful: "But if you insist on violence... fine. Just know that neither of us walks away from this owing nothing."

The giant's grip shifts on the morning star's haft. For just a moment, something in his eyes hesitates—an animal sense, old and wordless, telling him to leave. Then pride closes over his face like a shutter.

"Owing nothing?" he sneers. "Only thing owed tonight is that elf's blood."

The stranger lowers his gaze.

Something answers.

The hearthfire trembles slightly, as if something large had passed far underneath it. A faint draft moves through the room, though the front door has long been closed. It brushes against bare hands and stirs the edges of cloaks, carrying with it no scent of rain or night air. The flames dip for a moment, then steady themselves. Beneath the tables, shadows lie a little darker than before, quiet and unremarkable, as though they have always been waiting there.

The shimmer in his eyes is gone. What looks out from them now is patient in the way debts are patient.

"Oh," the giant says.

The stranger turns to the elf.

"I want you to understand something before this goes any further." His voice remains gentle. Friendly, even. "Everything I'm about to do, something will do back to me. Twice as hard, and probably in a way I'll regret. So believe me when I say—"

The air between them seems to draw heavy, as though the room itself has leaned in to listen.

"—I really hope you're worth it."
